{
  "slug": "2027-tax-brackets-projected",
  "title": "Projected 2027 Tax Brackets and Standard Deduction",
  "metaDescription": "Projected 2027 tax brackets: the single 10% rate tops out at $12,800 and 37% starts above $661,375. Estimates until the Internal Revenue Service publishes.",
  "h1": "Projected 2027 Tax Brackets and Standard Deduction by Filing Status",
  "cardBlurb": "Thomson Reuters Checkpoint projects the 2027 tax brackets and standard deduction, compared here with the Internal Revenue Service's 2026 figures.",
  "introText": "The projected 2027 tax brackets from [Thomson Reuters Checkpoint](https://tax.thomsonreuters.com/news/key-2027-figures-calculated-by-thomson-reuters-checkpoint-based-on-inflation-data-now-available/) keep seven rates from 10% to 37%: the single filer's 10% bracket tops out at $12,800, and the 37% bracket starts above $661,375. The [Internal Revenue Service (IRS)](https://www.irs.gov/filing/federal-income-tax-rates-and-brackets) has not published its 2027 figures, so every 2027 number here is a Checkpoint estimate. The 2027 brackets cover income earned during 2027 and apply to returns filed in 2028. A return filed in early 2027 uses the IRS's 2026 brackets. To turn either set of brackets into a dollar figure for your own income, see [how much tax you will pay](/guides/how-much-tax-will-i-pay/).",
  "sections": [
    {
      "heading": "Projected 2027 Tax Brackets by Filing Status",
      "body": "Thomson Reuters Checkpoint projects seven 2027 tax brackets for each filing status, taxed at 10%, 12%, 22%, 24%, 32%, 35%, and 37%. The dollar ranges apply to taxable income, which is your income after the standard deduction or other deductions.\n\nSingle filers:\n- 10%: $0 to $12,800\n- 12%: $12,800 to $52,025\n- 22%: $52,025 to $109,125\n- 24%: $109,125 to $208,325\n- 32%: $208,325 to $264,550\n- 35%: $264,550 to $661,375\n- 37%: over $661,375\n\nMarried filing jointly and surviving spouses:\n- 10%: $0 to $25,600\n- 12%: $25,600 to $104,050\n- 22%: $104,050 to $218,250\n- 24%: $218,250 to $416,650\n- 32%: $416,650 to $529,100\n- 35%: $529,100 to $793,650\n- 37%: over $793,650\n\nHead of household:\n- 10%: $0 to $18,250\n- 12%: $18,250 to $69,650\n- 22%: $69,650 to $109,100\n- 24%: $109,100 to $208,300\n- 32%: $208,300 to $264,550\n- 35%: $264,550 to $661,350\n- 37%: over $661,350\n\nThe joint thresholds are exactly double the single thresholds from the 10% bracket through the 32% bracket. For example, $12,800 times 2 is $25,600, and $264,550 times 2 is $529,100. The 37% bracket breaks that pattern: it starts at $793,650 for joint filers, far below double the single threshold of $661,375, which would be $1,322,750.\n\nHead of household filers get wider low brackets than single filers. Their 10% bracket runs $5,450 higher ($18,250 versus $12,800), and their 12% bracket runs $17,625 higher ($69,650 versus $52,025). From the 24% bracket up, the two schedules nearly match, and the 37% threshold differs by only $25."
    },
    {
      "heading": "Projected 2027 Standard Deduction",
      "body": "Thomson Reuters Checkpoint projects a 2027 standard deduction of $16,600 for single filers and $33,200 for married couples filing jointly. Each projected amount sits a few hundred dollars above the IRS's 2026 figure.\n\n- Married filing jointly and surviving spouses: $33,200 projected for 2027, up $1,000 from $32,200 in 2026\n- Single: $16,600 projected, up $500 from $16,100\n- Head of household: $24,900 projected, up $750 from $24,150\n- Married filing separately: $16,600 projected, up $500 from $16,100\n\nThe standard deduction comes off your income before any bracket applies. A single filer with $70,000 in wages would have $53,400 of taxable income on the 2027 projection ($70,000 minus $16,600), compared with $53,900 under the 2026 figure ($70,000 minus $16,100).\n\nAt a 22% top rate, that extra $500 of deduction is worth $110 in tax ($500 times 22%). At a 12% top rate, it is worth $60."
    },
    {
      "heading": "Why the 2027 Tax Brackets Are Estimates",
      "body": "The 2027 tax brackets are estimates because the IRS sets the yearly inflation adjustments itself, and as of October 2, 2026, it had not published them on irs.gov. Thomson Reuters Checkpoint released its projections on September 11, 2026, and labels them as estimates calculated from public data.\n\nCheckpoint based its math on Internal Revenue Code (IRC) section 1(f), the provision that ties the brackets to inflation. It used 11 months of published Consumer Price Index (CPI) data, running from September 2025 through August 2026.\n\nThe October 2025 CPI reading was never published because of the 2025 government shutdown. That missing month is one reason the IRS's final 2027 numbers could land a little above or below the projections. Checkpoint states the IRS will publish its figures later in 2026. The IRS has not announced a release date on the pages checked, and the 2026 adjustments came out in October 2025 in [Revenue Procedure 2025-32](https://www.irs.gov/newsroom/irs-releases-tax-inflation-adjustments-for-tax-year-2026-including-amendments-from-the-one-big-beautiful-bill)."
    },
    {
      "heading": "How the Projected 2027 Tax Brackets Compare with 2026",
      "body": "The projected 2027 tax brackets use the same seven rates as the IRS's 2026 brackets, with every dollar threshold moved higher. The low-end thresholds rise about 3.2%, so the same income falls a little lower in the schedule.\n\nSingle filers, top of each bracket (2026 IRS figure, then 2027 projection):\n- 10%: $12,400 to $12,800, up $400\n- 12%: $50,400 to $52,025, up $1,625\n- 22%: $105,700 to $109,125, up $3,425\n- 24%: $201,775 to $208,325, up $6,550\n- 32%: $256,225 to $264,550, up $8,325\n- 35%: $640,600 to $661,375, up $20,775\n\nMarried filing jointly, top of each bracket:\n- 10%: $24,800 to $25,600, up $800\n- 12%: $100,800 to $104,050, up $3,250\n- 22%: $211,400 to $218,250, up $6,850\n- 24%: $403,550 to $416,650, up $13,100\n- 32%: $512,450 to $529,100, up $16,650\n- 35%: $768,700 to $793,650, up $24,950\n\nFor the 2026 head of household thresholds, see the IRS [federal income tax rates and brackets](https://www.irs.gov/filing/federal-income-tax-rates-and-brackets) page. If your 2027 income rises by less than these thresholds do, part of it can move into a lower bracket than it would sit in under the 2026 schedule."
    },
    {
      "heading": "A Worked Example for a Single Filer Earning $70,000",
      "body": "A single filer with $70,000 in wages and only the standard deduction would owe $6,289.50 in federal income tax on the 2027 projections, $280.50 less than the $6,570 owed under the 2026 IRS figures.\n\nThe 2027 projection works out like this:\n- Taxable income: $70,000 minus the $16,600 standard deduction equals $53,400\n- 10% on the first $12,800: $1,280\n- 12% on the next $39,225 (from $12,800 to $52,025): $4,707\n- 22% on the last $1,375 (from $52,025 to $53,400): $302.50\n- Total: $6,289.50\n\nUnder the 2026 figures, the same $70,000 leaves $53,900 of taxable income after the $16,100 standard deduction, and the tax comes to $6,570. This filer's top bracket is 22% in both years. Only $1,375 of income is taxed at 22% on the projection, and the overall tax equals about 9% of the $70,000 in wages.\n\nTreat the example as an illustration. It ignores tax credits, payroll taxes under the Federal Insurance Contributions Act (FICA), state income tax, and any income besides wages. It also assumes wages stay at $70,000 in both years."
    },
    {
      "heading": "How to Use Projected Numbers for Planning",
      "body": "Use the projected 2027 numbers for rough planning, then replace them with the IRS's figures once they are published. The projections are close enough to sketch a budget and too early to file with.\n\n- Budgeting: run your expected 2027 income through the projected brackets and standard deduction to get a ballpark federal tax bill.\n- Withholding: compare that ballpark figure with what your paychecks will withhold, and plan a change if the gap is large.\n- Estimated payments: if you pay tax on income without withholding, use the projected tax as a starting estimate and rerun it after the IRS releases the 2027 adjustments.\n\nThese projections do not serve anyone filing a return for the 2026 tax year, which includes returns filed in early 2027. For that return, use the 2026 brackets in [Revenue Procedure 2025-32](https://www.irs.gov/newsroom/irs-releases-tax-inflation-adjustments-for-tax-year-2026-including-amendments-from-the-one-big-beautiful-bill). The projections also give no 2027 figures for capital gains rates, 401(k) or IRA limits, or the FICA wage base, so plan those from published numbers only.\n\nOne event would change these numbers: the IRS releasing its 2027 inflation adjustments. If the IRS figures differ from Checkpoint's, the IRS figures govern 2027 returns. If a lower projected tax frees up cash, the [retirement calculators](/retirement/) and the [pay off debt or invest](/guides/pay-off-debt-or-invest/) guide can help you decide where it goes. Your next step is to run your own 2027 income through the projected 2027 tax brackets above, then rerun it on the IRS [federal income tax rates and brackets](https://www.irs.gov/filing/federal-income-tax-rates-and-brackets) page once 2027 figures appear there."
    }
  ],
